-------------------------------------\nKrause–Zahlten conjecture on the cross number of finite abelian groups | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/329097/krause-zahlten-conjecture-on-the-cross-number-of-finite-abel)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; 2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2013–2014). ... Source: Bumsoo Kim, “The Cross Number of Minimal Zero-sum Sequences in Finite Abelian Groups”, arXiv:1410.6867 (2015).\n\nadditive-number-theory finite-abelian-groups zero-sum-sequences\n\n# Krause–Zahlten conjecture on the cross number of finite abelian groups\n\n13 years old · traced to 2013\n\nLet $G$G be a finite abelian group. For a sequence $S$S over $G$G, let $\\mathsf{k}(S)$k(S) denote its cross number, let $\\mathsf{k}(G)$k(G) be the maximum cross number of a zero-sum free sequence over $G$G, and let $\\mathsf{K}(G)$K(G) be the maximum cross number of a minimal zero-sum sequence over $G$G. If $G=\\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r}C_{p_i^{\\alpha_i}}$G=⨁i=1 r​C p i α i​​​ is written as a direct sum of prime-power-order cyclic groups, define\n\n$$\\mathsf{k}^{\\star}(G)=\\sum_{i=1}^{r}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_i^{\\alpha_i}}\\right),\\qquad \\mathsf{K}^{\\star}(G)=\\mathsf{k}^{\\star}(G)+\\frac{1}{\\exp(G)}.$$k⋆(G)=i=1∑r​(1−p i α i​​1​),K⋆(G)=k⋆(G)+exp(G)1​.\n\nKrause–Zahlten conjecture. The equality\n\n$$\\mathsf{K}(G)=\\mathsf{K}^{\\star}(G)$$K(G)=K⋆(G)\n\nholds for all finite abelian groups $G$G, and therefore\n\n$$\\mathsf{k}(G)=\\mathsf{k}^{\\star}(G)$$k(G)=k⋆(G)\n\nholds for all finite abelian groups $G$G as well. This is a central problem on extremal cross numbers of minimal zero-sum and zero-sum free sequences. The paper notes proofs for several families, including groups with restricted numbers of prime divisors in the exponent and groups of small rank, but states that a full proof remains far away.\n\nReferences\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2013–2014). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are ar