-------------------------------------------------------------\nProjective nested Cartesian codes - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Projective_nested_Cartesian_codes)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Neumann, Cicero Carvalho, Hiram H. ... Full work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.6819 ... Algebraic coding theory; cryptography (number-theoretic aspects) (11T71) Applications to coding theory and cryptography of arithmetic geometry (14G50) Geometric methods (including applications of algebraic geometry) applied to coding theory (94B27)\n# Projective nested Cartesian codes\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1007/S00574-016-0010-ZMaRDI QIDQ2399639zbMATH OpenOpenAlexFDO\n\nAuthors Victor G. L. Neumann, Cicero Carvalho, Hiram H. López\n\nPublication date 24 August 2017\n\nPublished in Bulletin of the Brazilian Mathematical Society. New Series (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.6819\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nReed-Muller type codesprojective codesaffine codesGröbner bases methods\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nAlgebraic coding theory; cryptography (number-theoretic aspects) (11T71) Applications to coding theory and cryptography of arithmetic geometry (14G50) Geometric methods (including applications of algebraic geometry) applied to coding theory (94B27)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Gadouleau_M in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AGadouleau_M+in%3Amath)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{1310.6008, author = {Peter J.~Cameron and Ben Fairbairn and Maximilien Gadouleau}, title = {{C}omputing in permutation groups without memory}, year = {2013}, eprint = {1310.6008}, note = {arXiv:1310.6008v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1310.6008, author = {Peter J.~Cameron and Ben Fairbairn and Maximilien Gadouleau}, title = {{C}omputing in permutation groups without memory}, year = {2013}, eprint = {1310.6008}, note = {arXiv:1310.6008v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nMemoryless computation is a new technique to compute any function of a set of registers by updating one register at a time while using no memory. Its aim is to emulate how computations are performed in modern cores, since they typically involve updates of single registers. The memoryless computation model can be fully expressed in terms of transformation semigroups, or in the case of bijective functions, permutation groups. In this paper, we consider how efficiently permutations can be computed without memory. We determine the minimum number of basic updates required to compute any permutation, or any even permutation. The small number of required instructions shows that very small instruction sets could be encoded on cores to perform memoryless computation. We then start looking at a possible compromise between the size of the instruction set and the length of the resulting programs. We consider updates only involving a limited number of registers. In particular, we show that binary instructions are not enough to compute all permutations without memory when the alphabet size is even. These results, though expressed as properties of special generating sets of the symmetric or alternating groups, provide guidelines on the implementation of memoryless computation.\n\n  * Computing in matrix groups without memory\n\nPeter J. Cameron, Ben Fairbairn, Maximilien Gadouleau\n\nOct 23 2013 cs.CC cs.DM math.GR arXiv:1310.6009v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1310.6009, author = {Peter J.~Cameron and Ben Fairbairn and Maximilien Gadouleau}, title = {{C}omputing in matrix groups without memory}, year = {2013}, eprint = {1310.6009}, note = {arXiv:1310.6009v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nMemoryless computation is a novel means of computing any function of a set of registers by updating one register at a time while using no memory. We aim to emulate how computations are performed on modern cores, since they typically involve updates of single registers. The computation model of memoryless computation can be fully expressed in terms of transformation semigroups, or in the case of bijective functions, permutation groups. In this paper, we view registers as elements of a finite field and we compute linear permutations without memory. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Peter J. Cameron (https://dblp.org/pid/c/PeterJCameron.html)\nciteturn4search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; CoRR abs/1310.6008 (2013)\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/CameronFG13\n\nPeter J. Cameron, Ben Fairbairn, Maximilien Gadouleau:\nComputing in permutation groups without memory. CoRR abs/1310.6008 (2013)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i3]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/CameronFG13a\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Fairbairn_B in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AFairbairn_B+in%3Acs)\nciteturn4search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{1310.6008, author = {Peter J.~Cameron and Ben Fairbai